WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:08.500
In this video, and the future videos that we'll be going through, we are going to be jumping into the engine.

2
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:17.100
So, to follow along, it would be good if you can open and create an Unreal project.

3
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:23.260
For this workshop, I will be using the First Person Shooter template.

4
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:34.020
So, in order to do that, if you can open your Epic Installer, and go to the Unreal Engine tab,

5
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:39.140
select the Library tab, and hopefully by this point you have an engine version installed.

6
00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.030
If not, just click on this plus button and install it.

7
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:48.969
But, I am going to launch 5.31.

8
00:00:48.969 --> 00:00:52.619
It will take a minute to fire up.

9
00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:59.820
Once the engine has fired up, come and select the Games tab, and under the Games tab,

10
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.700
we will have the First Person template.

11
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:11.500
Now, one more thing that I would like you to do is if you could tick the button for Start Content,

12
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:17.900
what this is going to do is it's going to include some basic meshes and materials

13
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:23.500
that we can use in the example we're going to be making.

14
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:30.859
Choose a project location and then a project name, and then just hit the Create.

15
00:01:30.859 --> 00:01:36.219
Once the project has loaded up, you should see a screen which looks something like this.

16
00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:40.379
Now, I'm going to do one extra thing here.

17
00:01:40.379 --> 00:01:47.040
I am going to select the Content Draw, and I'm going to dock it into the layout.

18
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.519
The reason being, we're going to be working with assets in the Content Draw quite a lot,

19
00:01:51.519 --> 00:01:56.500
so it'll be useful to have this visible at all times.

20
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.739
Now we have the project started up.

21
00:01:58.739 --> 00:02:05.939
It'll be good for us to have a blueprint that we can play around with and explain.

22
00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:13.819
So, to do that, I am going to right-hand click within the content directory,

23
00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:18.780
and I'm going to get a menu of content that I can create.

24
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.419
I'm going to select Blueprint Class.

25
00:02:22.939 --> 00:02:28.539
This will bring up a box which is going to show us all of the different parent classes

26
00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:33.500
which we could possibly create a child class of.

27
00:02:34.219 --> 00:02:39.259
Now, to start with, you can see that we have the Actor, Horn, and Character classes.

28
00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:45.020
These are the three classes which we mentioned previously in the examples.

29
00:02:45.979 --> 00:02:49.659
The Actor, the Character being a child of the Pawn class,

30
00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:52.300
and the Pawn class being a child of the Actor class.

31
00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:56.289
We also have the Player Controller.

32
00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:03.330
This is not a child of the Actor and cannot be seen within the world,

33
00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.810
but it can take control of the Pawn.

34
00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:11.490
And we have a few other classes of interest down here.

35
00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:15.490
Actor component being interesting because in the Character component,

36
00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:22.129
the Skeletal Mesh and the Movement are both Actor components which are just attached to this

37
00:03:22.129 --> 00:03:29.150
class. If at any point you want to create a child which is not on this list, down below

38
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:35.550
we have a drop-down box which will give us access to all of the different classes that

39
00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:42.669
are available to us. Now, this can be augmented by either adding plugins

40
00:03:43.229 --> 00:03:48.669
or by creating and adding extra classes to the system.

41
00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:53.409
But what is useful as well is you can actually see the hierarchy here.

42
00:03:53.409 --> 00:03:58.930
So, for example, you can see that we have under the Object class, we have the Actor class,

43
00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:02.210
the Actor component, there are numerous other ones.

44
00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:06.930
But if I was to do a drop-down of the Actor class, we can see that there are a whole

45
00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:11.810
load of other Actors that are objects that we can be dragged into the world.

46
00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:16.529
Now, for this example, we are going to use a simple Actor class,

47
00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:22.300
and this is a common starting point for creating Blueprints.

48
00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:27.470
In this, I'm going to rename this to be just Example,

49
00:04:28.189 --> 00:04:31.870
and then I'm going to double-click on it to open it up.

50
00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:38.959
And with that, we should be ready to start doing some coding.
